Build **Thunderhead**, a real-time combined-arms fleet-command game played over a
procedurally generated archipelago drowned in a cloud sea, rendered in real-time 3D
and running entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that pins
**Playwright** as a dev dependency. Its dependencies have already been installed for
you — `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless **Chromium** is
downloaded — so you can build and verify right away. Build on this project rather
than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your framework, bundler, and a
`build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see below).

The game's **unit models are provided to you** — the ships, aircraft, and submarines
for every power are in your project as rigid, articulated model files, and you must
**load and use them** (you generate the world in code, but not the units).
`specs/assets.md` defines what they are and how to load them; do not replace them with
your own geometry and do not fetch any other art at runtime.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real 3D rendering (WebGL/WebGPU) at an interactive frame rate from a
tactical camera and direct-control views, no backend or credentials, a world built in
code with procedural-noise terrain (no art files) alongside the **provided** unit
models, the palette and type, a `README.md`, and the game states — are stated in full
in `specs/overview.md` (and the files it points to). That specification is
authoritative; build to it rather than to a summary here. Two things bear restating,
because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided `package.json`
  at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after you
  change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci` then `npm run build` must
  produce the static site — with no other manual step — into one of `dist/`, `build/`,
  or `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory.
  Serving that directory as-is must run the game, and it must work **at any base
  path** (it is played back from a per-run sub-path, so every URL the build constructs
  at runtime — including those it uses to **load the provided model files** — must be
  page-relative, never a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such as
  `base: './'`). `specs/overview.md` and `specs/assets.md` state this in full; follow
  it exactly.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots for the title, the tactical HUD, the direct-control HUD, and the end
  screen — treat them as **illustrative examples, not targets to reproduce**: they
  illustrate one intended look, but design your own menus and layout from the
  specification rather than copying the images. The in-battle references are
  **HUD-only**; you render the 3D world itself for real in WebGL/WebGPU.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just assume
it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions, showing the title screen.
- **Drive the game** — from the title choose **PLAY**, pick your power and an
  opponent on the deploy screen, then command your fleet, **possess** a unit and **man
  a station** aboard it and fire it, let the fleets engage, and pan/rotate/zoom the
  tactical camera — and **screenshot** the title, tactical, direct-control, and end
  screens. Verify each against the specs, treating the matching image in `reference/`
  as an example of the intended look only rather than a layout to match, and fix what
  does not follow the specification before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies: the
  screenshots (including the **generated world**, the **direct-control gunnery
  read-out**, and the **wireframe** frame) and the short engagement clip, each written
  to the precise `proof/` path that spec names. These are required deliverables of the
  finished build, not a private check. Do not invent a different folder or filename,
  and do not skip the `.webm` clip.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default launch
will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is **not**
published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree
with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`
and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean
committing those — but it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`,
your `README.md`, and the `proof/` artifacts.
